Q1, Q2: a protocol library the application drives, which writes no files

Upstream's core library is an application of its own: RQS spawns a TCP
server task, an mDNS task and a task per connection, keeps the download
directory and the device name in process-wide statics, reports through
broadcast channels, and writes received files itself. That design is
where several of the findings live:

- Q1: a received file's name is the peer's `FileMetadata.name`, run
  through a MIME-based extension fix and then `PathBuf::push`ed onto the
  download directory. A name with `../` components leaves the directory,
  and an absolute name replaces it (push of an absolute path discards the
  base).
- Q2: the destination is chosen when the introduction arrives, by
  probing with `exists()` for a free "name (n).ext", and created with
  `File::create` only when the user accepts: in between anything can
  appear at that path, and `File::create` then truncates an existing
  file and follows a symlink.
- Files are written with blocking I/O on the runtime's threads; the
  sender reads files with blocking I/O too, checks `is_file()` and then
  opens the path (a FIFO swapped in blocks a runtime thread forever),
  sends past the announced size when a file grows, and loops forever
  sending empty chunks when it shrinks. While it sends it never reads
  the socket, so the receiver's keep-alives go unanswered and its
  cancel is not seen.
- The TCP server accepts without limit and from any address, gives
  every connection a task with no bound on its life, stops accepting for
  good at the first accept error (one EMFILE), and runs a send inside
  its accept loop, so nothing is received while something is sent.

Sukkula already has code for all of that (its inbox, spec S1 and S3;
its consent broker and limits), and every other protocol it speaks goes
through it. So the library becomes a protocol library that an
application drives:

- InboundRequest::new(socket) and OutboundRequest::new(endpoint_id,
  socket, device_name, payload) wrap a connection the application
  accepted or opened. read_frame (cancel-safe) and process_frame, or
  next_event, run the protocol one frame at a time and return events.
- The receiver reports InboundEvent::Introduction (the files and text as
  the sender described them, every field untrusted), then FileChunk
  (offset, bytes, last) for accepted files, Text, Cancelled or
  Disconnected. accept_transfer, reject_transfer, cancel_transfer and
  disconnection are the application's answers. The library creates,
  names and writes no file: Q1 and Q2 have no code left to live in, and
  the application decides where bytes go.
- The sender takes OutboundPayload::Files (name, size, MIME type of each)
  or ::Text, reports IntroductionSent, Accepted, Rejected, Cancelled and
  Disconnected, and is handed file bytes with send_file_chunk, which
  never sends past the announced size, and finish_file, which refuses to
  end a file short. Sending a text works (upstream had a TODO).
- MDnsServer::new takes the device name and type; running it is being
  visible, and not running it is being hidden. MDnsDiscovery reports the
  endpoint id from the service name and marks removals.
- Gone: RQS, manager.rs (TcpServer, SendInfo), channel.rs, errors.rs,
  the visibility watch, the download-directory and device-name statics,
  TransferMetadata and the other channel payload types, the MIME-based
  renaming, and with them the directories, sys_metrics (a git
  dependency), once_cell and mime_guess dependencies and tokio-util's
  task tracker. Read timeouts move to the application with the loop:
  it wraps read_frame, which the previous patches made cancel-safe.

The handshake, the frame limits, the size checks, consent-first and the
Wi-Fi refusal of the previous patches carry over unchanged.
